bodyText:
Tennessee lets a tenant end a residential lease early in a few situations:
- a victim of domestic abuse, sexual assault or stalking who meets the statute's conditions;
- in the Act counties, after fire or casualty that substantially impairs the unit (deemed unfit for human habitation by a governmental authority);
- outside the Act counties, when the building becomes untenantable without the tenant's fault, unless a written agreement says otherwise;
- a person with a physical disability accepted into a public housing facility (unless the landlord made significant accessibility changes), on written evidence of the acceptance;
- and servicemembers under federal law.

No Tennessee statute gives a general right to end a residential lease for the landlord's misrepresentation (the statutory remedy covers only small commercial and industrial leases), though general fraud and misrepresentation law still applies. No statute provides that leaving a prescribed statement out of the lease releases unpaid rent.
